.\" UNIX V7 source code: see /COPYRIGHT or www.tuhs.org for details.
.TH ICHECK 1M
.SH NAME
icheck \- file system storage consistency check
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /etc/icheck
[
.B \-s
] [
.B \-m
] [
.B \-d
] [
.B \-b
.I numbers
] [
.I filesystem
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Icheck
examines a file system,
builds a bit map of used blocks,
and compares this bit map against
the free list maintained on the file system.
If the file system is not specified,
.B /dev/rmd0
is checked.
The normal output of
.I icheck
includes a report of
.IP ""
The total number of files and the numbers of
regular, directory, block special and character special files.
.IP ""
The total number of blocks in use and the numbers of
single- and double-indirect blocks and data blocks.
.IP ""
The number of free blocks.
.IP ""
The number of blocks missing; i.e. not in any file
nor in the free list.
.PP
.B Note:
v7 reported single-, double-
.B and triple-indirect
blocks.
This machine's inode has
.B eight
disk addresses of which
.B two
are indirect \-
.I NADDR
is 8 and
.I NLEVEL
is 2, six direct, one single indirect and one double \- and there is no third level.
.IR /usr/include/sys/param.h
says why: one drive is 2000 blocks, and the single indirect alone addresses 512 of them.
.PP
The
.B \-s
option causes
.I icheck
to ignore the actual free list and reconstruct a new one,
rewriting the free-list chain blocks and then the super-block.
The file system should be dismounted while this is done;
if this is not possible (for example if
the root file system has to be salvaged)
.I icheck
prints
.B "***** BOOT UNIX (NO SYNC!) *****"
and stops, exactly as
.IR fsck (1M)
does, because the old, bad in-core copy of the super-block must not be written back
over the new one.
Notice also that
the words in the super-block
which indicate the size of the free list and of the
i-list are believed.
The
.B \-s
option
causes the normal output reports to be suppressed.
.PP
.B Note:
v7's
.B \-s
laid the new list out in the rotational pattern the super-block's
.I s_m
and
.I s_n
described.
This system's super-block has neither field and its disk has no such latency to hide,
so the list is rebuilt plainly and descending \- which is how
.IR mkfs (1M)
builds one and how
.IR fsck (1M)
salvages one, so all three leave a volume the same shape.
.PP
.B Note:
.B \-s
requires an explicit
.I filesystem
and will not use the default.
A salvage is aimed deliberately.
.PP
.B Note:
v7's
.B \-s
wrote the super-block's count of free i-nodes as
.BR zero .
That was harmless there, nothing in v7 maintaining the field; here the kernel maintains it
(\c
.IR /usr/src/kernel/alloc.c ),
so this
.I icheck
computes it, as
.IR fsck (1M)
does.
.PP
Following the
.B \-b
option is a list of block numbers;
whenever any of the named blocks turns up in a file,
a diagnostic is produced.
.PP
The
.B \-m
option names every missing block as well as counting them.
.B Note:
this option is in v7's program and not in v7's manual page.
.PP
The
.B \-d
option suppresses the duplicate check, and with it the block map and the
count of missing blocks.
It cannot be combined with
.BR \-s ,
which needs that map to know which blocks are free.
.PP
.B Note:
v7 said
.I icheck
"is faster if the raw version of the special file is used, since it reads the i-list many
blocks at a time".
Neither half holds here.
The raw device is the only useful target \- it is what
.B /dev/rmd0
above is \- and the transfer is
.B one block at a time
because
.I physio ()
permits nothing else: a raw transfer must be a whole number of 3072-byte blocks into a
buffer whose word address is a multiple of 512, at a block-aligned offset.
.SH FILES
/dev/rmd0
.SH "BLOCKS ARE 1024 BYTES"
.B "Note:"
the block
.I icheck
counts in is
.B 1024
bytes.
It is not this machine's filesystem block, which is
.B 3072
bytes \- 512 words of 48 bits \- and is three of them.
.PP
Two things follow.
Every count
.I icheck
prints is a
.B multiple of three,
the smallest thing that can be allocated being one 3072-byte block.
And the numbers are
.B half
what a PDP\-11 printed, v7 having counted in 512-byte blocks.
.PP
.B "Note:"
this applies to the
.BR used ,
.B free
and
.B missing
totals, which are measurements.
It does
.B not
apply to a block
.BR number ,
which is a quantity on the disk and is printed exactly as it stands there: the
.BR bad ,
.B dup
and
.B arg
diagnostics, the per-block lines under
.BR \-m ,
the block number in a free-list diagnostic, and the
.I s_isize
and
.I s_fsize
of a size check are all block numbers or descriptions of the volume.
So
.B "\-m"
prints a block number and then a total in different units, deliberately.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
dcheck(1M), ncheck(1M), clri(1M), fsck(1M), df(1M), mkfs(1M),
filsys(5)
.PP
.IR fsck (1M)
does all of this program's job and more, in one pass, and repairs what it finds.
.I Icheck
is worth reaching for when only the block accounting is wanted, or when the free list is to
be laid down again without answering any questions.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
For duplicate blocks
and bad blocks (which lie outside the file system)
.I icheck
announces the difficulty, the i-number, and the kind of block involved.
If a read error is encountered,
the block number of the bad block is printed and
.I icheck
considers it to contain 0.
`Bad free list' means that
a block number outside the available space, or an impossible count, was encountered in the
free list.
.PP
`\fIfilesystem\fR: not a filesystem' means the super-block carries no
.I FS_MAGIC
and `filesystem geometry mismatch' that it describes a block size, an i-nodes-per-block or
an addresses-per-inode this system does not have.
.B Note:
v7 had neither check, having no such fields;
.IR sbcheck ()
in the kernel refuses to mount such a volume and
.IR fsck (1M)
refuses to repair one.
.PP
`Can't get memory' means the block map would not fit.
.B Note:
v7 carried on at that point with duplicates unchecked, which silently turns
.B \-s
into a program that rebuilds a free list from an empty map.
It is a refusal here.
.SH BUGS
Since
.I icheck
is inherently two-pass in nature, extraneous diagnostics
may be produced if applied to active file systems.
.PP
.B Note:
v7's second bug here \- "it believes even preposterous super-blocks and consequently can get
core images" \- no longer applies: the magic number and the three geometry words are checked
before anything else is read.
